1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,456
The Saturn V is the rocket that

2
00:00:02,456 --> 00:00:04,357
took humans to the Moon,

3
00:00:04,357 --> 00:00:06,734
and it is covered in black and

4
00:00:06,734 --> 00:00:09,903
white stripes, like a giant barber pole.

5
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:12,649
Those markings are not decoration.

6
00:00:12,829 --> 00:00:15,985
The rocket stood one hundred eleven meters

7
00:00:15,985 --> 00:00:19,367
tall, weighed almost three million kilograms,

8
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:21,622
and every part of it was built

9
00:00:21,622 --> 00:00:23,050
for one single job,

10
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:25,530
getting three people to the Moon.

11
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:28,861
Today, nine parts of the Saturn V,

12
00:00:28,861 --> 00:00:31,085
and why each one exists.

13
00:00:31,436 --> 00:00:34,164
Ten Saturn V launches carried people.

14
00:00:34,344 --> 00:00:37,122
Nine took astronauts to the Moon,

15
00:00:37,122 --> 00:00:39,815
and one stayed in Earth orbit as

16
00:00:39,815 --> 00:00:40,404
a test.

17
00:00:40,584 --> 00:00:42,489
It was a machine so large that

18
00:00:42,489 --> 00:00:44,648
it needed a building to be stacked

19
00:00:44,648 --> 00:00:46,489
in, and yet nothing on it was

20
00:00:46,489 --> 00:00:47,632
there by accident.

21
00:00:47,981 --> 00:00:51,006
As always, every fact here comes from

22
00:00:51,006 --> 00:00:52,232
a named source,

23
00:00:52,232 --> 00:00:55,665
NASA documents and spaceflight references,

24
00:00:55,665 --> 00:00:58,117
all listed in the description.

25
00:00:58,297 --> 00:00:59,644
Let's start with the paint.

26
00:00:59,994 --> 00:01:02,891
Why was the Moon rocket painted black

27
00:01:02,891 --> 00:01:03,674
and white?

28
00:01:03,854 --> 00:01:05,595
Both colors had a job.

29
00:01:05,775 --> 00:01:08,790
The white paint reflected the sunlight,

30
00:01:08,790 --> 00:01:11,572
keeping the rocket cool while it sat

31
00:01:11,572 --> 00:01:13,814
on the pad in the hot Florida

32
00:01:13,814 --> 00:01:14,123
sun.

33
00:01:14,303 --> 00:01:17,437
A cooler tank means less fuel boiling

34
00:01:17,437 --> 00:01:19,469
off, and a safer rocket.

35
00:01:19,649 --> 00:01:23,054
The black markings helped ground cameras measure

36
00:01:23,054 --> 00:01:25,324
the roll, the spin of the rocket

37
00:01:25,324 --> 00:01:26,105
as it rose.

38
00:01:26,455 --> 00:01:29,355
The early Saturn rockets had more black

39
00:01:29,355 --> 00:01:30,843
than the later ones.

40
00:01:31,023 --> 00:01:32,358
On those flights,

41
00:01:32,358 --> 00:01:35,185
the fuel tanks under the black areas

42
00:01:35,185 --> 00:01:36,991
registered heat spikes,

43
00:01:36,991 --> 00:01:40,132
because the dark paint absorbed the heat

44
00:01:40,132 --> 00:01:40,996
of the Sun.

45
00:01:41,176 --> 00:01:43,466
So on later Saturn V rockets,

46
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:46,308
the upper black stripes and the band

47
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:49,150
on the first stage were painted over

48
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:49,860
in white.

49
00:01:50,211 --> 00:01:52,885
So the pattern on the rocket is

50
00:01:52,885 --> 00:01:54,611
the result of tests.

51
00:01:54,791 --> 00:01:56,401
Engineers painted it,

52
00:01:56,401 --> 00:01:58,317
watched the temperatures,

53
00:01:58,317 --> 00:02:00,004
and then repainted it.

54
00:02:00,184 --> 00:02:02,445
It is a good reminder that even

55
00:02:02,445 --> 00:02:05,145
the most famous design of the century

56
00:02:05,145 --> 00:02:06,604
was changed by data.

57
00:02:06,954 --> 00:02:09,473
Why does the bottom of the Saturn

58
00:02:09,473 --> 00:02:11,458
V have five giant engines?

59
00:02:11,638 --> 00:02:14,504
Because the rocket had to lift almost

60
00:02:14,504 --> 00:02:17,524
three million kilograms off the ground.

61
00:02:17,704 --> 00:02:20,593
The first stage had five Rocketdyne F-1

62
00:02:20,593 --> 00:02:23,334
engines, arranged in a cross with one

63
00:02:23,334 --> 00:02:24,371
in the middle,

64
00:02:24,371 --> 00:02:28,371
and together they produced about thirty-three thousand

65
00:02:28,371 --> 00:02:30,963
kilonewtons of thrust at sea level.

66
00:02:31,143 --> 00:02:33,511
That is about seven and a half

67
00:02:33,511 --> 00:02:34,696
million pounds.

68
00:02:35,046 --> 00:02:37,657
The first stage burned for only about

69
00:02:37,657 --> 00:02:39,492
one hundred fifty seconds.

70
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:41,785
In two and a half minutes,

71
00:02:41,785 --> 00:02:44,548
it used up most of its propellant,

72
00:02:44,548 --> 00:02:46,743
kerosene and liquid oxygen,

73
00:02:46,743 --> 00:02:49,262
and pushed the rocket up to the

74
00:02:49,262 --> 00:02:50,400
edge of space.

75
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:53,326
After that, the huge stage had done

76
00:02:53,326 --> 00:02:54,581
all it could do,

77
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:56,071
and it was dropped.

78
00:02:56,422 --> 00:02:58,999
The shape of that cross matters too.

79
00:02:59,179 --> 00:03:01,539
One engine in the center and four

80
00:03:01,539 --> 00:03:04,042
around it means a layout that stays

81
00:03:04,042 --> 00:03:06,545
balanced, and a design in which the

82
00:03:06,545 --> 00:03:09,120
outer engines can be tilted to steer

83
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:09,907
the rocket.

84
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:12,409
It is one of the most powerful

85
00:03:12,409 --> 00:03:14,498
engine clusters ever flown.

86
00:03:14,849 --> 00:03:16,846
Why is there a thin tower on

87
00:03:16,846 --> 00:03:18,773
the very top of the rocket?

88
00:03:18,953 --> 00:03:21,276
It is the Launch Escape System,

89
00:03:21,276 --> 00:03:23,675
a rocket motor built to save the

90
00:03:23,675 --> 00:03:24,049
crew.

91
00:03:24,229 --> 00:03:26,644
If the Saturn V ever started to

92
00:03:26,644 --> 00:03:28,591
fail on the pad or in the

93
00:03:28,591 --> 00:03:31,939
first minutes, the escape rocket would pull

94
00:03:31,939 --> 00:03:34,509
the crew capsule up and away from

95
00:03:34,509 --> 00:03:36,145
the exploding rocket,

96
00:03:36,145 --> 00:03:38,793
and the capsule would come down on

97
00:03:38,793 --> 00:03:39,961
its parachutes.

98
00:03:40,311 --> 00:03:42,036
It is a rocket on top of

99
00:03:42,036 --> 00:03:44,481
a rocket, designed to be used only

100
00:03:44,481 --> 00:03:46,278
if everything goes wrong.

101
00:03:46,458 --> 00:03:48,382
If everything went right,

102
00:03:48,382 --> 00:03:49,767
it was never used.

103
00:03:49,947 --> 00:03:51,319
On a normal flight,

104
00:03:51,319 --> 00:03:54,135
it was dropped shortly after the second

105
00:03:54,135 --> 00:03:55,291
stage took over,

106
00:03:55,291 --> 00:03:57,818
because by then it was dead weight.

107
00:03:58,169 --> 00:04:00,636
Every Apollo crew rode under that tower

108
00:04:00,636 --> 00:04:01,965
knowing it was there.

109
00:04:02,145 --> 00:04:03,781
It is a strange part,

110
00:04:03,781 --> 00:04:06,896
because its success is measured by never

111
00:04:06,896 --> 00:04:07,753
needing it.

112
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:10,551
A safety system that works is one

113
00:04:10,551 --> 00:04:12,693
that nobody ever sees fire.

114
00:04:13,043 --> 00:04:15,136
Why is there a ring between the

115
00:04:15,136 --> 00:04:15,608
stages?

116
00:04:15,788 --> 00:04:17,824
It is called the interstage,

117
00:04:17,824 --> 00:04:20,223
and it joins two stages that burn

118
00:04:20,223 --> 00:04:21,605
at different times.

119
00:04:21,785 --> 00:04:24,494
Its job comes at the next stage's

120
00:04:24,494 --> 00:04:25,233
ignition.

121
00:04:25,413 --> 00:04:28,757
About three minutes and twelve seconds into

122
00:04:28,757 --> 00:04:32,178
the flight, the interstage ring dropped away

123
00:04:32,178 --> 00:04:33,888
from the second stage.

124
00:04:34,068 --> 00:04:36,232
The ring was only about one meter

125
00:04:36,232 --> 00:04:37,739
from the outer engines,

126
00:04:37,739 --> 00:04:39,444
so it had to fall cleanly.

127
00:04:39,794 --> 00:04:42,351
The second stage engines had to light

128
00:04:42,351 --> 00:04:45,185
while still attached to the first stage's

129
00:04:45,185 --> 00:04:47,328
top, with the ring around them.

130
00:04:47,508 --> 00:04:48,981
When the ring dropped,

131
00:04:48,981 --> 00:04:50,988
the engines could burn freely,

132
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:53,464
with nothing in front of the nozzles.

133
00:04:53,644 --> 00:04:56,406
Any problem, and the second stage could

134
00:04:56,406 --> 00:04:57,185
be damaged.

135
00:04:57,536 --> 00:04:59,514
So a simple ring of metal had

136
00:04:59,514 --> 00:05:01,492
to be strong enough to hold a

137
00:05:01,492 --> 00:05:04,084
stage, and light enough to be dropped.

138
00:05:04,264 --> 00:05:06,566
And like many parts of a rocket,

139
00:05:06,566 --> 00:05:09,012
it worked perfectly or not at all.

140
00:05:09,362 --> 00:05:11,254
What is the ring near the top

141
00:05:11,254 --> 00:05:12,299
of the Saturn V?

142
00:05:12,479 --> 00:05:14,417
It is the Instrument Unit,

143
00:05:14,417 --> 00:05:15,834
the rocket's brain.

144
00:05:16,014 --> 00:05:18,075
It sat on top of the third

145
00:05:18,075 --> 00:05:20,295
stage and held the guidance,

146
00:05:20,295 --> 00:05:22,910
navigation and control equipment.

147
00:05:23,090 --> 00:05:26,041
It was designed at the Marshall Space

148
00:05:26,041 --> 00:05:28,512
Flight Center and built by IBM.

149
00:05:28,692 --> 00:05:31,423
The ring is six point six meters

150
00:05:31,423 --> 00:05:35,519
wide and only about ninety-one centimeters tall,

151
00:05:35,519 --> 00:05:39,188
and it weighs about two thousand kilograms.

152
00:05:39,538 --> 00:05:41,782
A rocket cannot be steered by a

153
00:05:41,782 --> 00:05:44,460
person from the ground at that speed.

154
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,209
So the unit measured where the rocket

155
00:05:47,209 --> 00:05:49,222
was, compared it to the plan,

156
00:05:49,222 --> 00:05:51,583
and told the engines how to point.

157
00:05:51,763 --> 00:05:52,567
In the end,

158
00:05:52,567 --> 00:05:55,053
the thing that put a spacecraft on

159
00:05:55,053 --> 00:05:56,881
its way to the Moon was a

160
00:05:56,881 --> 00:05:59,293
ring, a bit taller than a kitchen

161
00:05:59,293 --> 00:05:59,878
counter.

162
00:06:00,229 --> 00:06:02,574
It is almost funny that so much

163
00:06:02,574 --> 00:06:04,617
depended on so thin a part.

164
00:06:04,797 --> 00:06:07,218
But a computer is mostly the volume

165
00:06:07,218 --> 00:06:09,362
of wires and boxes that keep it

166
00:06:09,362 --> 00:06:11,576
alive, and the ring was just big

167
00:06:11,576 --> 00:06:12,959
enough to hold them.

168
00:06:13,309 --> 00:06:15,584
Why did the upper stages of the

169
00:06:15,584 --> 00:06:17,860
Saturn V burn a different fuel?

170
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:20,470
The first stage burned kerosene,

171
00:06:20,470 --> 00:06:22,748
which is dense and gives a lot

172
00:06:22,748 --> 00:06:24,495
of push for its volume.

173
00:06:24,675 --> 00:06:27,897
The second and third stages burned liquid

174
00:06:27,897 --> 00:06:30,097
hydrogen with liquid oxygen.

175
00:06:30,277 --> 00:06:33,594
Hydrogen gives much more energy for each

176
00:06:33,594 --> 00:06:37,244
kilogram, which matters most once the rocket

177
00:06:37,244 --> 00:06:40,064
is high and every kilogram counts.

178
00:06:40,244 --> 00:06:43,236
The second stage used five J-2 engines

179
00:06:43,236 --> 00:06:46,859
and burned for about three hundred ninety-five

180
00:06:46,859 --> 00:06:47,489
seconds.

181
00:06:47,839 --> 00:06:49,545
The price is the cold.

182
00:06:49,725 --> 00:06:52,205
Liquid hydrogen has to be kept at

183
00:06:52,205 --> 00:06:54,535
hundreds of degrees below zero,

184
00:06:54,535 --> 00:06:56,339
and it takes up a lot of

185
00:06:56,339 --> 00:06:56,714
room.

186
00:06:56,894 --> 00:06:59,208
That is why the second stage was

187
00:06:59,208 --> 00:07:00,076
a huge tank,

188
00:07:00,076 --> 00:07:02,318
nearly twenty-five meters tall,

189
00:07:02,318 --> 00:07:04,487
with so little else around it.

190
00:07:04,838 --> 00:07:06,853
So the Saturn V used the right

191
00:07:06,853 --> 00:07:08,936
fuel at each point of the trip.

192
00:07:09,116 --> 00:07:11,484
Dense fuel to leave the ground,

193
00:07:11,484 --> 00:07:14,082
and light, powerful fuel to go the

194
00:07:14,082 --> 00:07:15,304
rest of the way.

195
00:07:15,484 --> 00:07:18,461
Fuel was chosen the same way everything

196
00:07:18,461 --> 00:07:20,140
else was, for the job.

197
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:23,217
Why did the third stage fire two

198
00:07:23,217 --> 00:07:23,729
times?

199
00:07:23,909 --> 00:07:25,089
The first burn,

200
00:07:25,089 --> 00:07:27,999
about one hundred sixty-five seconds,

201
00:07:27,999 --> 00:07:31,146
put the spacecraft into orbit around the

202
00:07:31,146 --> 00:07:31,618
Earth.

203
00:07:31,798 --> 00:07:33,316
After a short pause,

204
00:07:33,316 --> 00:07:35,898
with the crew checking everything,

205
00:07:35,898 --> 00:07:38,479
the single J-2 engine lit a second

206
00:07:38,479 --> 00:07:41,896
time, for about three hundred twelve seconds,

207
00:07:41,896 --> 00:07:44,174
and sent them toward the Moon.

208
00:07:44,354 --> 00:07:47,524
This second burn is called the trans-lunar

209
00:07:47,524 --> 00:07:48,278
injection.

210
00:07:48,629 --> 00:07:51,071
Starting in orbit gave the crew time

211
00:07:51,071 --> 00:07:54,055
to check the spacecraft before committing to

212
00:07:54,055 --> 00:07:54,666
the Moon.

213
00:07:54,846 --> 00:07:56,501
If something was wrong,

214
00:07:56,501 --> 00:07:58,445
they could stay near Earth.

215
00:07:58,625 --> 00:08:00,282
If everything was fine,

216
00:08:00,282 --> 00:08:02,445
the second burn took them out.

217
00:08:02,625 --> 00:08:04,485
It was a safe way to start

218
00:08:04,485 --> 00:08:05,701
a very long trip.

219
00:08:06,052 --> 00:08:07,978
And after the second burn,

220
00:08:07,978 --> 00:08:10,498
the third stage had done its work.

221
00:08:10,678 --> 00:08:12,001
It stayed behind,

222
00:08:12,001 --> 00:08:14,257
while the crew went on in the

223
00:08:14,257 --> 00:08:15,113
spacecraft.

224
00:08:15,293 --> 00:08:18,540
The most powerful rocket ever flown by

225
00:08:18,540 --> 00:08:20,506
humans was, in the end,

226
00:08:20,506 --> 00:08:22,727
a way to push a very small

227
00:08:22,727 --> 00:08:25,974
capsule faster than anything had gone.

228
00:08:26,325 --> 00:08:29,471
Why was the first stage so enormous?

229
00:08:29,651 --> 00:08:32,857
It stood forty-two meters tall and weighed

230
00:08:32,857 --> 00:08:36,521
about two million two hundred thousand kilograms

231
00:08:36,521 --> 00:08:39,345
with fuel, most of the rocket's total

232
00:08:39,345 --> 00:08:39,879
weight.

233
00:08:40,059 --> 00:08:42,486
Almost all of that was propellant.

234
00:08:42,666 --> 00:08:44,985
The first stage had to carry its

235
00:08:44,985 --> 00:08:47,667
own fuel and the weight of everything

236
00:08:47,667 --> 00:08:50,204
above it, and the higher the rocket

237
00:08:50,204 --> 00:08:52,669
went, the less it needed to carry,

238
00:08:52,669 --> 00:08:54,988
so most of that mass was dropped

239
00:08:54,988 --> 00:08:56,366
at the first stage.

240
00:08:56,716 --> 00:08:59,583
This is the basic problem of rockets.

241
00:08:59,763 --> 00:09:01,027
To lift fuel,

242
00:09:01,027 --> 00:09:02,875
you need more fuel.

243
00:09:03,055 --> 00:09:05,905
A rocket that carries everything to the

244
00:09:05,905 --> 00:09:08,755
Moon at once would be impossibly heavy.

245
00:09:08,935 --> 00:09:10,963
So it is built in stages,

246
00:09:10,963 --> 00:09:13,883
each one throwing away the part that

247
00:09:13,883 --> 00:09:14,613
is empty.

248
00:09:14,963 --> 00:09:17,140
The Saturn V is the answer to

249
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:19,618
that problem at its most extreme.

250
00:09:19,798 --> 00:09:22,273
More than ninety percent of the rocket

251
00:09:22,273 --> 00:09:24,162
on the pad was never going to

252
00:09:24,162 --> 00:09:25,139
reach the Moon.

253
00:09:25,319 --> 00:09:27,265
It was only there to carry the

254
00:09:27,265 --> 00:09:28,303
last part of it.

255
00:09:28,653 --> 00:09:31,617
Why is the spacecraft so small compared

256
00:09:31,617 --> 00:09:32,681
to the rocket?

257
00:09:32,861 --> 00:09:34,946
Because it is the only part that

258
00:09:34,946 --> 00:09:36,054
goes to the Moon.

259
00:09:36,234 --> 00:09:37,832
The whole Saturn V,

260
00:09:37,832 --> 00:09:41,113
almost one hundred twelve meters of it,

261
00:09:41,113 --> 00:09:44,225
existed to move one small capsule and

262
00:09:44,225 --> 00:09:45,487
a lunar module.

263
00:09:45,667 --> 00:09:47,995
Nine of the ten crewed Saturn V

264
00:09:47,995 --> 00:09:51,600
flights took twenty-four astronauts to the Moon.

265
00:09:51,950 --> 00:09:54,614
Everything the astronauts needed,

266
00:09:54,614 --> 00:09:57,199
air, food, power and a way home,

267
00:09:57,199 --> 00:09:59,702
had to fit in that small space,

268
00:09:59,702 --> 00:10:02,205
and every kilogram of it had to

269
00:10:02,205 --> 00:10:04,628
be lifted by the rocket below.

270
00:10:04,808 --> 00:10:07,400
Engineers fought for every gram,

271
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:10,398
because each gram on the capsule cost

272
00:10:10,398 --> 00:10:12,261
far more on the ground.

273
00:10:12,612 --> 00:10:14,542
And that is the Saturn V in

274
00:10:14,542 --> 00:10:15,328
a nutshell.

275
00:10:15,508 --> 00:10:16,815
A tower of fuel,

276
00:10:16,815 --> 00:10:18,530
engines and machines,

277
00:10:18,530 --> 00:10:20,981
there to carry a small cone to

278
00:10:20,981 --> 00:10:23,349
a place that no one had been,

279
00:10:23,349 --> 00:10:24,820
and bring it back.

280
00:10:25,170 --> 00:10:26,633
So here are the nine.

281
00:10:26,813 --> 00:10:29,239
Paint that kept it cool and let

282
00:10:29,239 --> 00:10:31,197
cameras measure the roll,

283
00:10:31,197 --> 00:10:33,076
five engines in a cross,

284
00:10:33,076 --> 00:10:34,328
an escape tower,

285
00:10:34,328 --> 00:10:36,051
a ring that fell away,

